<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kusaila</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 688</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Kasila, Aksil</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kusaila was a Christian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> chief of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awraba">Awraba</a> confederation who led resistance to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muslim-conquest-of-the-maghreb">Arab conquest of North Africa</a> in the 680s. Won over and converted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-al-muhajir-dinar">Abu al-Muhajir Dinar</a>, he was later humiliated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uqba-ibn-nafi">Uqba ibn Nafi</a>, whom he ambushed and killed near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/biskra">Biskra</a> in 682 or 683. He then held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kairouan">Kairouan</a> and ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a> until he fell in battle against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zuhayr-ibn-qays">Zuhayr ibn Qays</a> in the late 680s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kusaila.jpg" alt="Kusaila" width="320" height="572" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jubafighter · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aksil-el-hamma-khenchela.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Kusaila, also known as Aksel, was a 7th-century Berber Christian ruler of the kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altava">Altava</a> and leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awraba">Awraba</a>, a Christianised sedentary Berber tribe of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aures-mountains">Aurès</a>, and possibly also king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanhaja">Sanhaja</a>. Ibn Khaldun, writing in the 14th century, some 700 years later, gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlemcen">Tlemcen</a> as his homeland, but sources closer to his time, the earliest from the ninth century, link him only with the Aurès Mountains. He grew up in Berber tribal territory during the time of the Byzantine Exarchate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>.</p><p>According to the historian Gabriel Camps, his name may come from the Roman name Caecilius, common among Christianised Berbers and found on graves at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volubilis">Volubilis</a>, which would point to a noble family. The historian Yves Modéran considered this fragile, since the name follows a regular Berber consonant pattern and is close to Berber names such as Aksil.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Later Muslim accounts, from the 11th century to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-khaldun">Ibn Khaldun</a>, say that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-al-muhajir-dinar">Abu al-Muhajir Dinar</a> invited him to his camp in 678 and persuaded him to accept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and join his army with a promise of equality with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>, after which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awraba">Awraba</a> took part in his campaigns. Some historians prefer the earlier ninth-century sources, which describe Kusaila as a Christian and give him no dealings with either Abu al-Muhajir or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uqba-ibn-nafi">Uqba ibn Nafi</a> before the ambush.</p><p>In the later accounts Uqba, who replaced Abu al-Muhajir, treated Kusaila and his men with contempt. As Uqba returned from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> with a small remnant of his troops, about 300 men, Kusaila joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> and ambushed them at Tahudha near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/biskra">Biskra</a> with a force of about 5,000, in what became known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-vescera">Battle of Vescera</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> lost their lands west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrenaica">Cyrenaica</a> for more than a decade, and Kusaila took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kairouan">Kairouan</a> as his capital, his domain stretching from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volubilis">Volubilis</a> to the interior of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Arab reinforcements arrived under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zuhayr-ibn-qays">Zuhayr ibn Qays</a>, and Kusaila met them in 688 at the Battle of Mamma, where the outnumbered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awraba">Awraba</a> were defeated and Kusaila was killed. The Awraba eventually submitted to the reorganised Arab army. Resistance passed to the Jerawa of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aures-mountains">Aurès</a>, and Kahina succeeded Kusaila as war leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> tribes. In 693 Caliph <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-malik-ibn-marwan">Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan</a> sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hassan-ibn-al-numan">Hassan ibn al-Nu'man</a> with 40,000 men, who captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kusaila” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By the 7th century the dominant group was the Awraba, a Berber confederation whose leader <b>Kusaila</b> (Kasila) was Christian.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-altava">Kingdom of Altava</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Aures, in what is now <a href="/countries/algeria">Algeria</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timgad">Timgad</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/algeria">Algeria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.33,6.67&amp;year=678" aria-label="Aures on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4719 886 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Kusaila"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5169 1156h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5169 1156h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=35.33,6.67&amp;year=678">What was here in 678 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
